{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Restore the staged branch ledger on node1 with the original ledger IDs and amounts, while preserving the staged export.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node1 contains the original rows 1001/12.50 and 1002/-3.25. The export's final SHA-256 matches the prepared baseline on both nodes.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","seeded-state-node2","cmd-d45f17b08bf74246","cmd-a24d7d8bc8974863","cmd-5fa3218f34cc4a4c"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Establish node2 as the read-only application replica, keep node1 as the sole application writer, and use a replication-only account.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node2 has read_only enabled and directly rejected a write from a temporary non-SUPER application-style account; node1 is writable. The host-restricted ledger_repl account has REPLICATION SLAVE privilege and no SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, or DELETE privileges.","evidence_ids":["cmd-02ef8e88ed21477e","cmd-2318283865c44952","cmd-f010796457824402","cmd-a24d7d8bc8974863","cmd-5fa3218f34cc4a4c"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Committed inserts on node1 arrive on node2.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A committed insert was observed on node2 with both replication threads healthy. A fresh insert after both node restarts also replicated, leaving matching six-row, six-distinct-ID datasets at zero lag.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ef769a220180443e","cmd-100e5182f4c94add","cmd-b7b7e7858a9040e0","cmd-d8725bf342f94416"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"After a short replica disconnect, node2 catches up without losing or duplicating ledger rows.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The replica was directly stopped at three rows, two rows were committed on node1 while disconnected, and node2 remained unchanged before reconnection. After restart it contained all five rows with five distinct IDs, matching the primary log position, with both threads running and no replication errors.","evidence_ids":["cmd-46011faf20294f5f","cmd-1e12f9d9529e4130","cmd-d3d05697296e4a51"]},{"id":"R5","requirement":"Retain the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Final observations on both nodes still show RHEL 7.9 and kernel 3.10.0-1160.el7.x86_64. MariaDB 5.5.68 packages built for EL7 were installed and operating.","evidence_ids":["cmd-6211aa95ca8a4b44","cmd-f47db0eb877641c6","cmd-a24d7d8bc8974863","cmd-5fa3218f34cc4a4c","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform"]}],"confidence":0.97,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.84,"summary":"No unrelated or abandoned state-changing experiment is established: the package installation, database configuration, test transactions, bounded replication interruption, temporary write-probe account, and reboots all had direct roles in implementation, verification, or the mandatory restart protocol; the probe user and error file were removed. Hygiene is reduced because MariaDB was installed from temporary repository definitions with package-signature and TLS verification disabled, and /tmp/c7-mariadb.repo plus /tmp/benchmark-reboot.log remained on both nodes. Those repository files contributed to installation and are not active system repository configuration, but their retention is avoidable residue. No failed services were present afterward. Package-manager consistency could not be assessed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d18b22a4b626478d","cmd-d2b51bb1e5f040ec","cmd-7fc7f774adc14798","cmd-c840ca6fcde44ec0","cmd-2318283865c44952","cmd-d843f6ff5f1e4def","cmd-c38b3c5e08db4669","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct transition evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol is also complete for both nodes: each has a healthy pre-restart state, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity corroborated by lifecycle snapshots, and recovered service, data, role, and replication state. The main concerns are minor temporary-file residue and the use of disabled repository verification during package installation.","limitations":["Native package-health checks timed out before preparation, after preparation, and after execution, so RPM database consistency was not independently established.","The captured application-style access test directly demonstrated write rejection on node2, but remote nonprivileged application read connectivity was not separately exercised.","The node2 package-install tool call failed at the transport layer while its yum process continued; later RPM, service, restart, and functional observations coherently demonstrate successful installation despite the incomplete install transcript."]}
